        "Chile is a wine country shaped by walls. To the east the Andes rise above 6,000 m; to the west the cold Humboldt Current chills the Pacific; to the north lies the Atacama, the driest desert on Earth; to the south, ice fields and fjords. Inside that box, a strip rarely wider than 180 km and more than 1,300 km long from Copiapó to Chiloé, sits one of the most naturally protected vineyards in the world. Phylloxera, the root louse that destroyed Europe's vineyards in the late 19th century, has never established itself here, so most Chilean vines still grow on their own roots, and Chile's quarantine rules for plant material remain among the strictest of any wine country.\n\n"
